<TopicPage slug="subjects/journalism/intermediate/interviewing">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The interview is journalism's <strong>primary research instrument</strong> — the UX lesson's
    behavioural questioning plus the research-methods lesson's discipline, aimed at
    people with reasons to manage you. Documents tell you what was written down;
    interviews produce what nobody wrote down — motive, sequence, reaction, the quote
    that only exists because you asked. The craft looks like conversation, but it is
    conversation with a purpose, a map and a contract: every interview is a negotiation
    over what may be published, conducted while you are also trying to learn the truth.
    Preparation earns access; listening earns the story. This page covers the
    preparation that makes an interview work, the anatomy of a good question, the
    follow-up and silence techniques that professionals actually rely on, the ladder
    of accountability questions, the ground-rules system (on the record to off the
    record), and the ethics of interviewing people at the worst moments of their lives.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="preparation, open questions, earned hard questions, ground rules" widget="equationStep" config={intCfg} />

  <h2>Preparation: the interview is won before it starts</h2>
  <p>
    The professional rule is blunt: <strong>never ask a question the public record can
    answer</strong>. Reading everything first — past coverage, filings, speeches, the
    company's own marketing — does three jobs at once. It tells you what the subject
    has already committed to (so you can check new statements against old ones: the
    contrast between the 2021 prospectus and the 2024 testimony is often the story);
    it signals respect, which buys cooperation; and it frees the live interview for
    what only the subject can supply. From that reading you build a <strong>question
    list</strong> — and then, paradoxically, plan to abandon it. The list is a map, not
    a script: the interview's value comes from following unexpected answers down their
    own roads, and an interviewer marching through questions misses the turn. Practical
    preparation has a checklist flavour: confirm the logistics (who, where, how long,
    recording consent), identify the two or three facts you must have before you leave,
    and decide the <strong>ask</strong> — what exactly are you seeking (a quote for an
    established story? an explanation? a reaction to a document?). Also prepare the
    <strong>evidence folder</strong>: the documents you may need to place in front of the
    subject, printed and tabbed. Accountability interviews run on paper.
  </p>

  <h2>The anatomy of a question</h2>
  <p>
    Questions come in families, and each family buys something different.
    <strong>Open questions</strong> ("Tell me about…", "What happened next?", "How did you
    decide…?") invite narrative; they are how you get story, sequence and the
    unplanned. <strong>Closed questions</strong> ("Did you approve the payment?") pin facts
    and create commitments; they are how you lock a position on the record.
    <strong>Leading questions</strong> ("So you knew the building was unsafe, didn't you?")
    are mostly a mistake — they hand the answer over and produce worthless confirmation;
    the honest form of the same move is to <em>present the evidence</em> and ask for the
    response ("Here is the 2019 inspection report flagging the cladding — what did you
    do when you read it?"). The classic errors to strip from your own style:
    <strong>double-barrelled questions</strong> ("Was it negligence or budget pressure?" —
    the subject answers whichever half is easier), <strong>opinions disguised as
    questions</strong> (a speech, not a question), and <strong>stacked questions</strong>
    (three at once; the subject answers the one they like). A well-formed question is
    short, single and concrete — and then the craft moves to what happens after it.
  </p>

  <h2>Follow-ups and silence: where the story lives</h2>
  <p>
    The first answer is the press-release version; the <strong>follow-up</strong> is where
    journalism happens. The professional's toolkit is embarrassingly small:
    <em>"Why?"</em> (asked twice, it reaches motive), <em>"What do you mean by that?"</em>,
    <em>"Can you give me an example?"</em>, <em>"You said X earlier and Y just now — how do
    those fit together?"</em>, and the echo — repeating the subject's last words back as
    a question. The most under-used tool is <strong>silence</strong>: people are uncomfortable
    with conversational gaps and fill them, and what they volunteer to fill silence is
    unprepared material — often the sentence they had decided not to say. Hold two
    seconds after an answer ends before you move on; the material that arrives in the
    gap is frequently the interview's lede. A worked rhythm from a profile interview:
    the subject says the launch "was chaos". The novice moves to the next question.
    The professional says nothing, or says "chaos?" — and gets: "We had two servers and
    one engineer, and on the morning of the launch the database crashed for four
    hours." The first answer was a claim; the second is a story with numbers in it.
  </p>

  <h2>Rapport and accountability: earning the hard questions</h2>
  <p>
    Interviews divide by intent. The <strong>cooperative interview</strong> (profile,
    explainer, human interest) runs on rapport: genuine curiosity, open questions,
    the subject talking more than you. The <strong>accountability interview</strong> (the
    subject who may have done wrong, or is responsible for a failure) is a different
    instrument used at a different time. Its rules: the hard questions come
    <em>after</em> the basic ones, so the subject has committed to a record you can
    test against; the confrontation is with <strong>evidence, not attitude</strong> — a
    document on the table, a timeline read aloud; and the request is always for
    <em>response</em>, which is both the fairness duty (no serious allegation is
    published without a chance to answer) and a reporting technique, because the
    response is frequently newsworthy in itself: the denial that contradicts the
    document, the non-denial ("we will not comment on personnel matters"), the
    refusal to be interviewed (itself publishable, with the timestamps). The
    broadcast convention of the "no-surprises" letter or call — telling the subject
    in advance which allegations you intend to publish — balances fairness with
    the need not to tip off someone who might destroy evidence; newsrooms differ
    on where they draw the line, and the reasoning is worth studying.
  </p>

  <h2>Ground rules: on the record, on background, off the record</h2>
  <p>
    Attribution levels are the interview's contract, and the professional discipline
    is to <strong>negotiate them before the answer, never after</strong> — a source who
    says "this is off the record" <em>after</em> speaking has, strictly, no claim to it,
    and the graceful move is to say so and then negotiate terms anyway. The ladder,
    roughly as major outlets teach it: <strong>on the record</strong> — everything usable,
    attributed by name and title. <strong>On background</strong> (or "not for attribution") —
    the information is usable but the source is described by role ("a senior Treasury
    official"), which still narrows the pool: agree the description's wording with the
    source. <strong>Deep background</strong> — information you may use to guide reporting
    but may not quote or attribute in any way; it points at documents and other
    sources. <strong>Off the record</strong> — for your understanding only, not
    publishable; most reporters grant this sparingly, because you cannot verify what
    you cannot use, and because sources routinely use the term hoping it means
    "publishable but anonymous". That ambiguity is itself the lesson: <strong>terms mean
    what the two of you agree they mean</strong>, so define them aloud. The ethical spine
    underneath: promises to sources are kept absolutely (breaking one ends your
    career at that source, and word travels), and anonymity is granted for protection
    of the source, never for their convenience or your access.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> a good interviewer is a fast, forceful talker —
  the television image. The craft runs the other way: the best interviewers speak
  least, listen hardest, and make subjects feel heard precisely so they will keep
  talking. Force closes people down and produces the defensive sound bite; patience
  produces the second, third and fourth answers, which are the publishable ones.</p>

  <h2>Recording, quotes and the truth of the tape</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Record with consent</strong> (laws vary by jurisdiction — in general, consent
    from one party to the conversation is the floor in the UK and much of the US, but
    newsroom policy and honesty set the higher standard), and take notes anyway —
    recorders fail, and the notebook carries your observations. The tape protects
    both sides: every disputed quote resolves to the recording, and "I was
    misquoted" is the most common complaint in journalism. On quotes themselves the
    professional line is strict: quotes are verbatim; you may tidy stumbles that do
    not change meaning ("gonna" → "going to"), you may not rearrange, trim to flip
    sense, or clean up grammar into something the person did not say. And never
    read quotes back for approval — fact-check facts with sources if you must,
    but the quote, once on the record, is theirs and is published as said.
  </p>

  <h2>Interviewing the vulnerable: trauma-aware practice</h2>
  <p>
    Interviewing survivors — of violence, disaster, bereavement — is a specialism
    with its own ethics, codified in resources like the Dart Center's guidance.
    The duties: <strong>informed consent</strong> (explain what publication means and
    reach, before the person has invested in telling you); <strong>control</strong>
    (let the survivor choose the setting, the pace, whether to take breaks; they
    may stop at any time); <strong>no gratuitous detail</strong> (do not press for
    re-traumatising specifics for colour — ask what they are willing to tell, and
    let that set the boundary); <strong>no ambush with the worst moment</strong>
    (approach through neutral ground: "tell me about the morning" rather than
    opening on the event); and <strong>dignity in the edit</strong> (what was said
    in a fragile moment may deserve more care than what was technically
    quotable). This is not softness versus rigour — traumatised witnesses who
    are treated well give fuller, more accurate accounts, and those who are
    re-traumatised disengage. The craft lesson generalises: the interview is a
    relationship with a purpose, and the purpose is served by the subject's trust
    more than by the interviewer's control.
  </p>

  <h2>The afterlife of an interview</h2>
  <p>
    The craft does not end when the subject leaves. <strong>Process the recording
    immediately</strong> — transcribe (machine transcription now drafts it;
    human ears verify quotes and the moments the machine mangles), while
    memory is fresh and deadlines allow. <strong>Write the source file</strong>:
    who the person is, what they are positioned to know, what they want,
    what they would not say, and the exact terms agreed — the file that
    makes the second call six months later warm instead of cold.
    <strong>Log the commitments</strong>: every promise made (terms, the
    return of documents, a look at nothing) goes in writing to yourself,
    because a promise you forgot is a promise you broke. And
    <strong>mine the negative space</strong>: the questions the subject
    deflected, the topics that changed the temperature, the answer
    given to a question you did not ask — deflection patterns are
    themselves reportable, and often become the follow-up
    interview's opening line. A paper trail is also self-protection:
    when a source disputes a quote or a term, the notebook,
    the recording and the file are the reporter's whole defence —
    the verification lesson's tape discipline, applied to
    relationships.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The PEACE model.</strong> UK policing replaced confrontational
    interrogation with the PEACE framework (Preparation and Planning; Engage
    and Explain; Account, probe and challenge; Closure; Evaluation), built on
    memory research: open prompts produce more accurate recall than pressure,
    and pressure produces false confessions. Investigative journalists borrowed
    it — the journalist's accountability interview and the detective's
    interview differ in purpose but share the finding that non-leading,
    free-recall-first questioning gets the truth out more reliably than the
    confession-movie style.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Gatekeepers and the access economy.</strong> Most senior subjects come
    through press officers, who trade access for control (questions in advance,
    approved quotes, limited time). The professional responses are known:
    document the attempt (the unanswered request is publishable), use the PR's
    written answers only as claims to be checked, and build the story on
    documents and multiple sources rather than on the one flattering sit-down.
    The deeper tension — access journalism versus accountability journalism,
    who gets interviewed at all — is a live debate in the field, sharpened by
    politicians who now reach the public without journalists entirely.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> the UX research and psychology methods lessons; the Dart Center's trauma-interviewing guidance; the PEACE model literature; SPJ ethics on sources.</p>

